function CourseResults({ courses, filter }: Props) {
  const [visible, setVisible] = useState(filterCourses(courses, filter));
  return <CourseGrid courses={visible} />;
}

After the course list refetched, React DevTools shows for CourseResults:

props
  courses: Array(20)
  filter: { category: "sql", search: "" }
hooks
  1 State: Array(19)

A console.log placed inside filterCourses prints on every render of CourseResults.

Statement: "Because filterCourses(courses, filter) runs on every render, `visible` is recalculated with the 20 new courses, so the missing course must be a problem in the API cache rather than in this component."

function CourseResults({ courses }: { courses: Course[] }) {
  const [selectedId, setSelectedId] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const selected = courses.find((c) => c.id === selectedId) ?? null;
  return (
    <>
      <CourseGrid courses={courses} onSelect={(c) => setSelectedId(c.id)} />
      {selected && <CourseDetails course={selected} />}
    </>
  );
}

The learner selects course c_12. An instructor renames it, and the courses query refetches:

GET https://api.codedev.test/courses -> 200 [..., { "id": "c_12", "title": "React Hooks in Depth", ... }, ...]

The parent re-renders CourseResults with the new array.

Statement: "The details panel now shows \"React Hooks in Depth\" with no extra code, because only the id is kept in state and the course object is looked up again from the new array on this render."
